          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is described as first-line treatment for chronic insomnia?",
          "answer": "According to the sleep medicine and internal medicine guidance (*Chronic insomnia: cognitive behavioral therapy as first-line treatment*), cognitive behavioral therapy for insomnia (CBT-I) is the first-line treatment for chronic insomnia in adults, ahead of medication. The guidance says its effects last after treatment ends, while medication effects generally do not, and it avoids the risks linked to long-term sleep medicines.\n\nThe therapy is usually delivered as a package over about four to eight sessions. Its parts include:\n- **Stimulus control:** using the bed only for sleep, and getting up when unable to sleep.\n- **Sleep restriction:** limiting time in bed to match actual sleep time, then extending it gradually as sleep improves.\n\nThe same document also notes that alcohol can worsen sleep even when people use it to help them sleep.\n\nThe retrieved passages did not cover how CBT-I is delivered in specific settings, how to access it, or the other components of the program. A clinician can help you decide whether it fits your situation.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
